// io_read_n_bytes — SELF-HOSTED `io.read_n_bytes(n)` over the WASI stdin-N-bytes floor
// (prim.read_n_bytes), the v1 trust-spine realization of `io.read_n_bytes`. Reads UP TO `n` bytes
// of standard input (fd 0) and returns them as a `List[Int]` (each byte 0..255 as one element);
// fewer than `n` at EOF. The SIBLING of io_read_line (which builds a String); this builds a
// List[Int]. The prim packages the chunked fd_read loop and the list construction; the result is a
// fresh OWNED List[Int] (a flat block — its i64 elements own no nested handles, so the scope-end
// Drop frees just the block: no leak). Because this body reaches prim.read_n_bytes, its cap_witness
// carries Capability::Stdin — the SAME distinct capability io.read_line carries — so a caller is
// caps-verified ONLY if it declares Stdin (an `effect fn`). NON-DETERMINISTIC (reads live stdin):
// no byte-match unless stdin is fixed. Matches io.almd's `fn read_n_bytes(n: Int) -> List[Int]`.
//
// THERE IS NO PER-CALL MAXIMUM. The answer is min(n, what stdin has), and a caller who
// asks for 100 MiB of a 100 MiB stream gets 100 MiB.
//
// A cap shipped here briefly and it was wrong: it truncated the answer at 2^26 silently,
// on BOTH legs, for a call that had worked correctly on both. The measurement behind it
// was taken with FIVE BYTES on stdin — a large `n` failed there because this floor
// pre-allocates the REQUESTED size, not because the data could not be delivered.
//
// What IS real is the floor's i32 boundary: `prim.read_n_bytes` takes an i32, so an `n`
// that does not fit arrives truncated (i64::MAX became -1 and read nothing). The answer
// is to never hand the floor more than it can represent AND allocate — so this reads in
// 2^26 chunks and concatenates until it has `n` or the stream runs short. The chunk size
// bounds the wasm buffer; it does not bound the ANSWER. Keep it equal to
// ALMIDE_IO_READ_CHUNK_BYTES (runtime/rs/src/io.rs); the gate checks that pair.
//
// A short chunk means EOF, so the recursion stops there rather than spinning on an
// exhausted stream.
// `remaining <= 0` is the base case and it is NOT decoration: without it the exact-fit
// path recurses forever. A caller asking for exactly one chunk's worth reaches
// remaining = 0, where `want` is 0 and the short-read test `0 < 0` is FALSE — so the
// "keep going" branch is taken with remaining unchanged, and the recursion never ends.
// It cost a wasm `alloc` trap on `read_n_bytes(3)` to find, which is the cheapest input
// there is.
fn __read_n_chunked(remaining: Int) -> List[Int] = if remaining <= 0 then prim.read_n_bytes(0)
else {
  let want = if remaining > 67108864 then 67108864 else remaining
  let got = prim.read_n_bytes(want)
  if list.len(got) < want then got else got + __read_n_chunked(remaining - want)
}

fn io_read_n_bytes(n: Int) -> List[Int] = if n <= 0 then prim.read_n_bytes(0) else __read_n_chunked(n)
